# Line endings, and the files that must not have them rewritten.
#
# `* text=auto` is the general policy. Note it deliberately leaves a file that
# is ALREADY committed with CRLF alone, so adding an entry here does not
# retroactively convert anything; only an explicit `text` does, via
# `git add --renormalize`. The pins below follow the platform's file.
#
# Go: gofmt writes LF and reports a CRLF file as unformatted, so a formatting
# check would fail on files nobody touched. That is why CI can run one.
#
# Shell and YAML: a CRLF copy fails on Linux with `bad interpreter` or
# `$'\r': command not found`, and inside a container that surfaces as an
# immediate exit with no useful message.
* text=auto

*.go    text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf

*.png binary

# The generated SPA bundle, which git must not rewrite a byte of.
#
# This is not a tidiness preference. Minified chunks carry raw CR bytes inside
# string literals, inlined from dependency sources: ScanView's chunk holds 20
# of them and QrLabelModal's holds 8. They are data, not line endings, and
# `sed 's/\r$//'` leaves every one of them alone. Any eol conversion here would
# silently corrupt that data, and the damage would reach whoever ran the
# committed binary rather than whoever converted it.
#
# A consequence worth knowing, because it shapes the CI check: the bundle is
# NOT byte-reproducible across platforms. vite 8 builds through rolldown, whose
# native binding ships per platform, so a Windows build and a Linux build of
# the same sources differ in a handful of those embedded bytes. The content
# hashes in the filenames are identical, which is why CI compares the set of
# emitted chunks rather than their bytes.
internal/webui/public/** -text
